<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Intermediate Period</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Intermediate Period was the era of political fragmentation in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> between the Middle Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> ruled the north. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom describe how rulers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> expelled the Hyksos and reunified Egypt. This experience of foreign rule encouraged the militarism of the New Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period.jpg" alt="Second Intermediate Period" width="320" height="653" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iry-Hor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Hyksos_Period.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Second Intermediate Period is the time when ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was divided into smaller dynasties for the second time, dated from 1782 to 1550 BCE, between the end of the Middle Kingdom and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Egyptologists do not agree on how to define it. It is best known as the period when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, a people of West Asia, established the 15th Dynasty and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>. Manetho's Aegyptiaca says the dynasty was founded by a king named Salitis and presents the Hyksos as invaders of an obscure race who conquered Egypt with brutality, although their settling may have occurred peacefully.</p><p>The primary source for its chronology is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a>, from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, together with the study of scarabs, beetle-shaped amulets often inscribed with the names of rulers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The 12th Dynasty ended in the late 19th century BCE with the death of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a>, who left no heirs, and the weaker 13th Dynasty followed, ruling for most of its existence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itjtawy">Itjtawy</a>. It moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, possibly in the reign of Merneferre Ay, and after it withdrew its southern garrisons the rising Nubian state of Kush reoccupied the forts. A provincial family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> broke away to form the 14th Dynasty, and in the north the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> kings of the 15th Dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> for about a hundred years, and Avaris may have been the largest city in the world from 1670 to 1557 BCE. Whether the Hyksos came as an army or as a mass migration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> is still debated. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> the 16th Dynasty ruled the Theban region and was eventually subsumed around 1580 BCE. A short-lived Abydos Dynasty may have ruled part of Upper Egypt in the meantime. The Thebans then founded the 17th Dynasty, which made a short peace with the Hyksos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The peace ended in the reign of Seqenenre, who began a series of wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> and died in battle. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamose">Kamose</a> continued the war, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> dealt the final blow, expelled the Hyksos, united Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and became the first king of the 18th Dynasty and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><p>Advances of the period were adopted by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, among them the horse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khopesh">khopesh</a> sickle sword, although the credit given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> for the chariot is contested. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> adopted Egyptian royal titles and hieroglyphic inscriptions and worshipped the Egyptian pantheon, taking Set as their patron god. A strontium isotope analysis favoured a migration model over an invasion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Intermediate Period of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Kingdom began with the expulsion of the Hyksos, an Asiatic dynasty that had controlled Lower Egypt for over a century during the <b>Second Intermediate Period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt was divided among the Hyksos, a Theban line in the south and probably a short-lived Abydos Dynasty, opening the <b>Second Intermediate Period</b>, until Theban kings reunited the country and founded the New Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When Egyptian power weakened in the <b>Second Intermediate Period</b>, Kerma reached its height, absorbed the kingdom of Sai and allied with the Hyksos.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Book of the Dead itself first developed in Thebes toward the beginning of the <b>Second Intermediate Period</b>, around 1700 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
